-> Custom connection every thing all :  Authentication type : 

-> API key: The 3 rd party will have a default API key for their platform and using it, we can pass the API key and send the request.  -> Basic Authentication: It’s like password and user name , where we need to pass the parameter as a password and user name to accept the request

->Oauth1, Oaith 2: The same principle 

Parameter Type:  -> Query String: Suppose the API key needs to be passed as a Query String then it would be in this way:  The authentication value is added to the URL as a query parameter  GET https://api.example.com/customers?api_key=abc123xyz

-> Header:  The authentication value is included in the HTTP request headers  GET https://api.example.com/customers
api_key: abc123xyz
-> Form Data:  Authentication parameters are sent in the HTTP request body as form-encoded data or multipart form data, depending on the configuration and API requirements.
Example: URL-encoded form body

POST https://api.example.com/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

client_id=APP123&client_secret=SECRET456

2. Compare them side by side
Feature	Query String	Header	Form Data
Location	URL	HTTP headers	Request body
Typical example	?api_key=123	x-api-key: 123	api_key=123 in body
Common use	URL-based API parameters	API keys and authorization tokens	Form-encoded parameters
Requires a request body?	No	No	Yes
Main caution	Secrets can leak through URL logs	Must use the correct header name and format	Must match the expected body encoding

Which one should you select?
Use this simple guide:
Choose Query String if the API documentation shows:
https://api.example.com/data?api_key=YOUR_KEY

Choose Header if it shows:
Authorization: Bearer YOUR_TOKEN
or x-api-key: YOUR_KEY.

Choose Form Data if the API explicitly requires credentials or parameters in an application/x-www-form-urlencoded or supported multipart request body.

