->Okay, what is the difference between :

Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded, application/json,multipart/form-data ?

Imagine you're sending the same information to an API: 
* Name: Ravi 
* Age: 25 
* City: Chennai

application/x-www-form-urlencoded-> The data is sent in the below way:  name=Ravi&age=25&city=Chennai

Common uses: login forms, simple form submissions, and OAuth token requests. 
application/json-> The data is sent in the below way:  {
  "name": "Ravi",
  "age": 25,
  "city": "Chennai"
}

Common uses: REST APIs, creating or updating records, sending complex data, and passing arrays or nested objects. 
multipart/form-data-> this is different and this is used to send the files  Some kind of example, bit not exactly:  formData = Map();
formData.put("name", "Ravi");
formData.put("city", "Chennai");

response = invokeurl
[
    url: "https://api.example.com/users"
    type: POST
    parameters: formData
    headers: {"Content-Type":"multipart/form-data"}
];

info response;

Common uses: uploading images, documents, attachments, or sending files alongside ordinary form fields.


4. Which one should you choose?
Scenario	Content type
API expects simple form fields	application/x-www-form-urlencoded
API expects a JSON object	application/json
API accepts file uploads or multipart forms	multipart/form-data
OAuth token endpoint requires form-encoded parameters	application/x-www-form-urlencoded
API requires a nested object or array in JSON	application/json

